3|0@unitedkingdom^1440072694#cu9dq52:TheConnivingPedant$:Because he wasn't *supposed* to win. Politicians tend to be ambitious people; they want power, they want to be important, they want to have a trail of people kissing their arse and calling them 'minister', (or better yet *'prime minister'*!) So a run at the leadership and therefore a shot at becoming PM is a once-in-a-lifetime chance at the Big Job, the one they've been dreaming of since they got elected president of their Oxbridge college JCR. And to this end they plan their careers, build their power bases and polish their images. Some politicians don't care about that and are happy to be backbenchers. They can concentrate on representing their constituents, arguing for what they believe and they *don't* have to toe the party line because they don't care about being rewarded with government positions. That's Corbyn; he was not on the "leadership career track", not only was he a principled backbencher, but one who's held his principles for so long that they've gone out of and come back into fashion. Speaking of fashion... Well lets just say that he's the antithesis of a clean-cut, ambitious politician. To someone like Chuka Umunna who (and I don't mean this disparagingly) looks like he would be just as comfortable on some trading floor in Canary Wharf as in the House of Commons, this sort of thing is not supposed to happen - it's the world turned upside down. It's like your old, embarassingly excentric uncle who somehow managed to get out of his room and shows up while you're trying to entertain and impress some Very Important People and sits himself down at the head of the table. It's not supposed to happen and it's *ruining everything*! I think that's why some people in the party are in a panic - and it is panic. They're used to playing the game according to a certain set of rules and assumpitons about what you should do and how you should look when doing it and they're being wiped out by someone just doesn't care about the game or its rules.
